WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.019
- Good morning, Believe Nation!

00:00:01.019 --> 00:00:01.877
It's Evan.

00:00:01.877 --> 00:00:04.332
My one word is believe,
and I believe in people

00:00:04.332 --> 00:00:06.135
more than they believe in themselves.

00:00:06.135 --> 00:00:08.935
And my sincere hope is
that if you see in yourself

00:00:08.935 --> 00:00:12.246
what I see in you, you'll be
able to change the planet.

00:00:12.246 --> 00:00:14.260
So to help you on your
journey, today's message is

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.374
The Customer is King.

00:00:16.374 --> 00:00:18.130
Over to you, Gordon Ramsay.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:20.008
(rooster crowing)

00:00:20.008 --> 00:00:22.879
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:22.879 --> 00:00:25.505
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:25.505 --> 00:00:29.504
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:29.504 --> 00:00:31.390
- Key to a successful business

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:33.762
is remembering the customer's king.

00:00:33.762 --> 00:00:36.502
Because without them, we're nobody.

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:39.150
And so I, from a chef's point of view,

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.011
always put myself in a
customer's situation,

00:00:41.011 --> 00:00:43.270
where I see it from their eyes.

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:44.643
I don't cook for chefs.

00:00:44.643 --> 00:00:46.002
Every time chefs come
into the dining room,

00:00:46.002 --> 00:00:47.513
first thing they do is turn
all the plates upside-down

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:49.767
and start photographing the food.

00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:53.785
And they're constantly
dissecting the food,

00:00:53.785 --> 00:00:55.398
as opposed to enjoying it.

00:00:55.398 --> 00:00:58.529
So, I always look at it from
a customer's perspective,

00:00:58.529 --> 00:00:59.910
because they're king.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.625
And without them, we're history.

00:01:02.625 --> 00:01:04.758
- So, when I first took
over Toronto Dance Salsa

00:01:04.758 --> 00:01:06.864
just over a year ago...

00:01:06.864 --> 00:01:08.138
Wow, almost two years ago.

00:01:08.138 --> 00:01:10.264
How long has it been, Alex?

00:01:10.264 --> 00:01:11.140
January.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.250
January last year, and it's August.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:14.256
So, yeah.

00:01:14.256 --> 00:01:15.635
Wow, it's been awhile!

00:01:15.635 --> 00:01:17.652
So, I took over Toronto Dance Salsa,

00:01:17.652 --> 00:01:19.263
and I sat down with Alex,

00:01:19.263 --> 00:01:21.003
I hired Alex away from his full time job

00:01:21.003 --> 00:01:22.636
to work at Toronto Dance Salsa.

00:01:22.636 --> 00:01:23.511
And I said,

00:01:23.511 --> 00:01:25.015
"okay, how are we going
to grow this business?"

00:01:25.015 --> 00:01:26.513
Here are 10 ways that we
can grow our business,

00:01:26.513 --> 00:01:28.513
I gave him a list of 10
things that he could do.

00:01:28.513 --> 00:01:30.148
Because if you try to do all 10 things,

00:01:30.148 --> 00:01:31.766
you're going to suck at all of them.

00:01:31.766 --> 00:01:33.380
I think we try to do
that as entrepreneurs,

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.486
we try to do everything,

00:01:34.486 --> 00:01:37.077
and then nothing gets done properly.

00:01:37.077 --> 00:01:38.824
And so, we had 10 things on our list,

00:01:38.824 --> 00:01:41.205
10 ways to market our
business and to grow it.

00:01:41.205 --> 00:01:42.409
He had to pick two.

00:01:42.409 --> 00:01:43.961
Now one, I picked for him.

00:01:43.961 --> 00:01:46.647
One was being a thought leader,
one was sharing content,

00:01:46.647 --> 00:01:47.991
one was sharing his opinions,

00:01:47.991 --> 00:01:50.659
being on Snapchat, being
a leader, making videos,

00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:52.643
that had to be part of the plan.

00:01:52.643 --> 00:01:54.263
If you're going to work under me,

00:01:54.263 --> 00:01:55.898
if we're going to create
this relationship,

00:01:55.898 --> 00:01:57.002
if I'm going to mentor you,

00:01:57.002 --> 00:01:58.888
being a thought leader
has to be a part of it.

00:01:58.888 --> 00:02:00.262
That's why Alex is in some of the videos,

00:02:00.262 --> 00:02:01.489
you've seen him on Entspresso,

00:02:01.489 --> 00:02:02.441
you've seen him on #SevenWays,

00:02:02.441 --> 00:02:03.399
you see him a lot.

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:04.630
The second way, you pick!

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.647
Pick the thing that
resonates the most with you.

00:02:06.647 --> 00:02:08.269
And the one that he picked

00:02:08.269 --> 00:02:11.507
was having a remarkable
customer experience.

00:02:11.507 --> 00:02:13.769
So that anytime somebody
came into our studio,

00:02:13.769 --> 00:02:15.513
or anybody, somebody visited our website,

00:02:15.513 --> 00:02:18.896
or wrote in to our team,
or tried to call us,

00:02:18.896 --> 00:02:21.509
they would have an amazing
customer experience.

00:02:21.509 --> 00:02:24.026
Because if the customer loves the service,

00:02:24.026 --> 00:02:25.478
they love the experience,

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:27.385
they're going to come back,
they're going to be more loyal,

00:02:27.385 --> 00:02:28.774
and they're going to tell their friends.

00:02:28.774 --> 00:02:31.688
And so, we made the decision to invest

00:02:31.688 --> 00:02:34.544
our resources, our time,
our energy, our dollars,

00:02:34.544 --> 00:02:38.544
on how do we make the experience
better for our customers.

00:02:38.544 --> 00:02:43.052
And that extends to everything
that we do in the business.

00:02:43.052 --> 00:02:46.008
So, it's not just about the
service that we provide, right?

00:02:46.008 --> 00:02:48.382
People come here, and they
want to learn dance classes.

00:02:48.382 --> 00:02:50.251
Great, you want to give them
a good experience, right?

00:02:50.251 --> 00:02:51.885
So, they're in class,
they're learning the steps,

00:02:51.885 --> 00:02:53.294
they're learning the moves, great.

00:02:53.294 --> 00:02:55.283
Understanding why they came here.

00:02:55.283 --> 00:02:57.245
Trying to tap into the deeper reason,

00:02:57.245 --> 00:02:59.261
and then give them a better experience

00:02:59.261 --> 00:03:01.899
that hits the core of why they
signed up in the first place.

00:03:01.899 --> 00:03:04.022
Most people don't come
trying to learn dance moves,

00:03:04.022 --> 00:03:06.087
they're coming because they need a break

00:03:06.087 --> 00:03:07.395
from their crappy life.

00:03:07.395 --> 00:03:09.377
They hate their job, they're
trying to meet somebody,

00:03:09.377 --> 00:03:10.882
they just broke up with somebody,

00:03:10.882 --> 00:03:12.886
they're looking for some new friends,

00:03:12.886 --> 00:03:14.126
it's a thing to do.

00:03:14.126 --> 00:03:15.390
And I want to turn the business

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.887
from not just being something
that is a thing to do,

00:03:17.887 --> 00:03:19.392
to cross off your bucket list,

00:03:19.392 --> 00:03:21.012
but something that has a meaningful,

00:03:21.012 --> 00:03:24.259
lasting, long-term impact on
the lives of our customers.

00:03:24.259 --> 00:03:27.333
So, you then expanded
out to the entire chain

00:03:27.333 --> 00:03:29.279
of a lifetime of a customer.

00:03:29.279 --> 00:03:31.513
So, when did they first write in to us?

00:03:31.513 --> 00:03:33.147
Or do they call in to us?

00:03:33.147 --> 00:03:35.056
How is that first conversation going?

00:03:35.056 --> 00:03:37.789
What can be better about the
email that we respond to?

00:03:37.789 --> 00:03:39.307
Or how can we pick up the phone faster,

00:03:39.307 --> 00:03:41.992
or give them a better
experience on the phone?

00:03:41.992 --> 00:03:42.933
Then they come to the class,

00:03:42.933 --> 00:03:44.208
and then what happens after class,

00:03:44.208 --> 00:03:45.962
and what happens in between the classes?

00:03:45.962 --> 00:03:47.393
And so, using the lens, right?

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:49.413
This is how we look at our customers

00:03:49.413 --> 00:03:50.265
lookin' at our business,

00:03:50.265 --> 00:03:52.761
how do we create a more
remarkable experience?

00:03:52.761 --> 00:03:54.894
You can go through every little step,

00:03:54.894 --> 00:03:56.009
every little touch point,

00:03:56.009 --> 00:03:59.004
every little interaction
that a customer has with us,

00:03:59.004 --> 00:04:00.760
and a customer has with you,

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.491
you could find a way to make it better.

00:04:02.491 --> 00:04:05.765
And think of it not just as
providing a great service,

00:04:05.765 --> 00:04:07.001
or providing a great product,

00:04:07.001 --> 00:04:08.729
not just giving them what they paid for,

00:04:08.729 --> 00:04:13.242
but the experience in dealing
with you has to be amazing.

00:04:13.242 --> 00:04:17.043
So an example, one of the things
when we're in a phone call,

00:04:17.043 --> 00:04:18.140
people have questions.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:19.971
And it's a lot of the
standard, same questions.

00:04:19.971 --> 00:04:21.248
But it's not just about

00:04:21.248 --> 00:04:23.133
answering their questions
and being friendly,

00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:25.266
but it's always lookin'
for a way to over-deliver.

00:04:25.266 --> 00:04:26.756
If you understand their why,

00:04:26.756 --> 00:04:28.324
that they're signing up,
why they're interested,

00:04:28.324 --> 00:04:30.382
it's looking for something
else that you can offer

00:04:30.382 --> 00:04:32.276
at the end after
answering their questions,

00:04:32.276 --> 00:04:34.528
that can be that extra bonus to them

00:04:34.528 --> 00:04:37.386
where they leave with
their life and their day

00:04:37.386 --> 00:04:40.764
feeling better, because of the
interaction they had with us.

00:04:40.764 --> 00:04:42.639
And so, if you look at your experience,

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:44.948
look at every touch point
you have with your customer,

00:04:44.948 --> 00:04:46.646
think about how you can not just

00:04:46.646 --> 00:04:48.383
provide them with the answers,

00:04:48.383 --> 00:04:50.464
not just provide them
with a great solution

00:04:50.464 --> 00:04:51.640
that solves their problem.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.808
But the entire experience of
dealing with your customers

00:04:55.808 --> 00:04:58.151
is not just a pleasant, positive one,

00:04:58.151 --> 00:04:59.759
but is an outstanding one.

00:04:59.759 --> 00:05:02.014
That they want to rave
about their friends to,

00:05:02.014 --> 00:05:04.378
that they can't wait to come
back and deal with you again,

00:05:04.378 --> 00:05:06.757
and they want to spread the
mission, spread the word for you.

00:05:06.757 --> 00:05:08.509
So the question of the
day today is, I'm curious,

00:05:08.509 --> 00:05:10.756
what is something that you do

00:05:10.756 --> 00:05:13.305
to really wow your
customers above and beyond

00:05:13.305 --> 00:05:15.004
just giving them a great
product or service?

00:05:15.004 --> 00:05:15.837
I'd love to hear from you,

00:05:15.837 --> 00:05:17.008
maybe learn something as well

00:05:17.008 --> 00:05:18.633
that I can apply to my businesses.

00:05:18.633 --> 00:05:20.247
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:05:20.247 --> 00:05:22.205
Also, if you leave a comment
within the first couple hours,

00:05:22.205 --> 00:05:24.927
you have a chance to win
one of our two daily prizes.

00:05:24.927 --> 00:05:27.263
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Neal Kerr.

00:05:27.263 --> 00:05:28.642
Neal, thank you so much for picking up

00:05:28.642 --> 00:05:30.281
a copy of my book, Your One Word.

00:05:30.281 --> 00:05:33.253
And making that YouTube
video, and posting it online.

00:05:33.253 --> 00:05:34.738
I really appreciate the support, man,

00:05:34.738 --> 00:05:37.165
and I'm so glad that you enjoyed my book.

00:05:37.165 --> 00:05:39.393
Thank you guys again for
watching, I believe in you!

00:05:39.393 --> 00:05:41.393
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:05:41.393 --> 00:05:43.384
and whatever your one word is!

00:05:43.384 --> 00:05:45.647
Much love, and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:05:45.647 --> 00:05:48.408
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:05:48.408 --> 00:05:50.942
♫ I wake up every morning

00:05:50.942 --> 00:05:54.629
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:05:54.629 --> 00:05:57.526
(typing)

00:05:57.526 --> 00:05:58.585
- I used to work for Warren Avis,

00:05:58.585 --> 00:06:00.255
the guy who started Avis Rent a Car.

00:06:00.255 --> 00:06:01.440
And he said to me one day,

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.278
"Robert, what's the purpose of business?"

00:06:03.278 --> 00:06:04.111
And I said,

00:06:04.111 --> 00:06:04.944
"oh, make a profit."

00:06:04.944 --> 00:06:05.918
And all these other things, and he said,

00:06:05.918 --> 00:06:09.623
"no, the purpose of business
is to create a customer."

00:06:09.623 --> 00:06:10.456
He goes,

00:06:10.456 --> 00:06:12.016
"Everything else takes care of itself.

00:06:12.016 --> 00:06:15.607
"If you can create customers,
and create value for them,

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:17.564
"because the answer to
that question is different

00:06:17.564 --> 00:06:19.479
"for every single customer,

00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:21.262
"then you're going to
have a great business."

00:06:21.262 --> 00:06:22.555
- I like to sit down with founders,

00:06:22.555 --> 00:06:26.399
and I like to work on removing
friction from process,

00:06:26.399 --> 00:06:28.015
whatever it might be.

00:06:28.015 --> 00:06:30.387
And creating products that are intuitive.

00:06:30.387 --> 00:06:32.817
And one of the things
that I get from working

00:06:32.817 --> 00:06:34.768
in the creative space, and being an actor

00:06:34.768 --> 00:06:39.490
is really understanding
feeling, and touch, and art.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:40.860
And you know, it's an art.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:45.651
Being able to create an
impulse within a consumer

00:06:45.651 --> 00:06:48.137
to do something is an art form.

00:06:48.137 --> 00:06:49.640
I could probably give
you 10 or 20 examples

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:52.756
of companies that have
really just taken software

00:06:52.756 --> 00:06:57.255
that was archaic software that
was untouchable by consumers,

00:06:57.255 --> 00:07:00.151
and actually brought it
down to a consumer level.

00:07:00.151 --> 00:07:02.617
That is becoming the process

00:07:02.617 --> 00:07:05.675
to building a great
software company these days.

00:07:05.675 --> 00:07:07.453
You could look at a company like Zenefits.

00:07:07.453 --> 00:07:09.686
Where it's like, there's
always been HR software

00:07:09.686 --> 00:07:12.211
that's been out there in
some way, shape, or form.

00:07:12.211 --> 00:07:13.536
Or you could look at Robinhood.

00:07:13.536 --> 00:07:15.983
And there's always been
ways that you can interface

00:07:15.983 --> 00:07:17.946
and do stock exchanges.

00:07:17.946 --> 00:07:21.830
But making it something
that the average consumer

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:25.455
can intuitively understand,
and touch, and play with,

00:07:25.455 --> 00:07:28.384
and use, and have it be
beneficial in their life,

00:07:28.384 --> 00:07:30.876
is about understanding the intuition

00:07:30.876 --> 00:07:33.398
and the feeling of the consumer.

00:07:33.398 --> 00:07:35.877
- I thought that right now
would be the perfect time

00:07:35.877 --> 00:07:40.069
to share a customer
service story with you.

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:40.986
True story.

00:07:42.367 --> 00:07:46.741
Couple years ago, I got
a call from a young lady.

00:07:46.741 --> 00:07:47.765
And was all excited,

00:07:47.765 --> 00:07:49.630
she tells me she's looking for a property,

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:52.380
and then she says the word lease.

00:07:54.215 --> 00:07:56.691
Our favorite words as realtors.

00:07:56.691 --> 00:07:59.676
(audience laughing)
"I'm looking for a lease."

00:07:59.676 --> 00:08:03.468
(sighs) You know, unless it's
an $85,000 a month lease,

00:08:03.468 --> 00:08:04.551
I don't know.

00:08:06.778 --> 00:08:09.248
But I was in a good mood,
so I took that call.

00:08:09.248 --> 00:08:10.081
And I said,

00:08:10.081 --> 00:08:12.599
"Sure, I'll help you find a lease."

00:08:12.599 --> 00:08:16.349
And it turns out, it was
a $5,000 a month lease.

00:08:16.349 --> 00:08:18.635
And I took her around

00:08:18.635 --> 00:08:20.546
for six months.

00:08:20.546 --> 00:08:22.923
(audience laughing)

00:08:22.923 --> 00:08:24.862
The term was only a year.

00:08:24.862 --> 00:08:26.925
(audience laughing)

00:08:26.925 --> 00:08:30.470
Anyways, I like to finish what I start.

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:33.779
So, I went through with it,
found her an amazing place,

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:35.869
she couldn't be happier.

00:08:35.869 --> 00:08:37.097
In fact, she was so happy,

00:08:37.097 --> 00:08:39.505
that she called her father
that night, and she said,

00:08:39.505 --> 00:08:41.042
"Dad, I got the greatest place!

00:08:41.042 --> 00:08:42.975
"My realtor's amazing!

00:08:42.975 --> 00:08:44.991
"He doesn't care about money at all!"

00:08:44.991 --> 00:08:47.991
(audience laughing)

00:08:49.767 --> 00:08:53.260
And it happened to be that her father

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:56.856
was sitting next to
the CEO of his company.

00:08:56.856 --> 00:09:00.492
And he heard the story, and that CEO said,

00:09:00.492 --> 00:09:03.409
"Actually, I'm thinkin'
of selling my penthouse.

00:09:03.409 --> 00:09:05.731
"Maybe I should use that guy!"

00:09:05.731 --> 00:09:07.884
Sure enough, I went in, I met the CEO,

00:09:07.884 --> 00:09:12.445
and I got the listing of
his $4,000,000 penthouse.

00:09:12.445 --> 00:09:16.155
(audience clapping)

00:09:16.155 --> 00:09:18.687
While I was sitting that open house

00:09:18.687 --> 00:09:20.117
on that $4,000,000 penthouse,

00:09:20.117 --> 00:09:22.265
cause that's my favorite thing to do,

00:09:22.265 --> 00:09:23.674
(audience laughing)

00:09:23.674 --> 00:09:26.245
a celebrity walked in.

00:09:26.245 --> 00:09:27.678
And that celebrity walked around,

00:09:27.678 --> 00:09:30.509
and he checked out that
incredible penthouse.

00:09:30.509 --> 00:09:33.543
And he looked at me and he said,

00:09:33.543 --> 00:09:36.999
"yeah, this is cool, do
you have anything cooler?"

00:09:36.999 --> 00:09:38.135
(audience laughing)
I said,

00:09:38.135 --> 00:09:38.968
"Sure."

00:09:40.078 --> 00:09:41.519
So I went back to the office,

00:09:41.519 --> 00:09:43.223
and I put together the list

00:09:43.223 --> 00:09:46.640
of the coolest penthouses you could find.

00:09:48.124 --> 00:09:50.843
And I showed that celebrity around,

00:09:50.843 --> 00:09:55.010
and that celebrity ended up
buying an $11,000,000 penthouse.

00:09:56.384 --> 00:09:59.711
Which broke the record for Santa Monica.

00:09:59.711 --> 00:10:03.544
So from that lease, that
$5,000 a month lease,

00:10:04.476 --> 00:10:07.729
which led to that
$4,000,000 penthouse sale,

00:10:07.729 --> 00:10:10.805
which led to that
$11,000,000 penthouse sale,

00:10:10.805 --> 00:10:14.070
and it is the gift that keeps on giving,

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:15.653
on, and on, and on.

00:10:17.111 --> 00:10:19.444
That is why customer service

00:10:20.745 --> 00:10:24.009
is so important to everybody's business.

